  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    Nyla confessed on the clearest sky anyone could remember, aurora-light falling through the Lens-Cathedral in shafts of green and violet that struck the ice floor like verdicts. Frost-ward Yunjoushong zhu'Eyrie stood in the auditor's gallery and watched the Shadow-Broker speak for three hours without pause, her ash-gray wrappings wrong against the crystal geometry of the chamber, her voice steady as a penguin reciting tide-tables. Smuggling routes. Fire-core diversions. The murder of her predecessor—throat opened to match a claw-mark, body fed to a vent. Thirty winters of the Midnight Claws' operations, laid out with the precision of a ledger.
    
    Amaang Skael'Compass leaned toward Yunjoushong from the council tier above. "Thorough," Amaang said. The word carried approval—the particular Aurora Stack approval that meant *this is handled now.*
    
    But Yunjoushong was an auditor. She counted things. And what she counted was this: Nyla mentioned ☽'Ilun the Refracted exactly once, in passing, as "a complication resolved." No details. No method. No motive beyond *he learned too much.* Then she moved to the next item as if the death of an ice-reader who'd spent three seasons studying the Thaw-Paths Medallion was a line-item between fire-core quotas and tunnel rights.
    
    Clear skies for the confession. Yunjoushong thought of the saying and wondered what it cost in dark.
    
    Afterward, the council chambers emptied with the careful satisfaction of officials who've witnessed something unpleasant come to a clean end. Amaang stopped Yunjoushong at the gallery stairs. "The Exchange wants verification. You'll audit her claims against our records—routes, quantities, dates. Standard work."
    
    "And the murder?"
    
    Amaang's flippers settled into a posture that could have been patience or concealment. "She confessed. The method matches the evidence. Verify what can be verified."
    
    Yunjoushong took the assignment. She also took the long way home through the Lens-Cathedral's lower gallery, past the high chamber where the Thaw-Paths Medallion sat under Stillness-Bound. She didn't touch it. She looked. Aurora-glass the color of old ice, carved pathways interlocking, small enough to hide in a closed flipper. This late, no aurora-light reached it.
    
    It was glowing anyway. Faint. Steady. Showing paths to no one.
    
    ---
    
    The courier who called himself Splitpath met her at Midway Shelf, where the seventeen skeletons stood in their frost-locked poses—flippers bound behind them, beaks open in the last shape their faces ever made. He glanced at them the way couriers always did: brief, deliberate, the way you'd check the edge of a blade.
    
    "Sealed crates," Yunjoushong said. "The Claws ran diverted fire-cores through your routes. Nyla's confession lists dates."
    
    "She would." Splitpath's flippers bore the courier ink—glowing faintly in the tunnel half-light, marking him as inviolable. "She always knew our schedules better than we did."
    
    "Did the weights match?"
    
    A pause. Splitpath had the look of someone calculating what truth cost. "No," he said. "Not in the last two seasons before she confessed. Crates were heavier. We reported it to the Exchange."
    
    "To whom at the Exchange?"
    
    "Council tier. Amaang Skael'Compass took the report."
    
    Yunjoushong wrote this down. Wrote it very carefully.
    
    In the Nightshelf, she found Obscurum~ in a dead-forge corridor that still smelled of rendered fat and the wrong kind of heat. The Midnight Claws member—former member, if the distinction mattered—sat with a bone-clasp in one flipper, turning it, turning it. His bioluminescent markings had gone dull. When Yunjoushong asked about ☽'Ilun the Refracted, his marking flickered once.
    
    "You want to know what he found," Obscurum~ said.
    
    "I want to know why he died."
    
    "Same answer. Different weight." He set down the bone-clasp. "☽'Ilsancuia Depth-walker spent a decade polishing that medallion because it was *suffering,* she said. When she died—old age, lucky creature—☽'Ilun picked it up. He wasn't as gentle. He used ice-reading techniques the Stack doesn't teach anymore. Forced the medallion to show its pathways under conditions it didn't want."
    
    "What did the pathways show?"
    
    Obscurum~ made a sound—not a laugh, closer to the noise ice makes before it shears. "Routes. Approach vectors through the Stack's eastern ridge. Mapped in the glass like old scars. And a second map underneath, in a different light, that showed where the defenses *weren't.*" He picked up the bone-clasp again. "☽'Ilun told someone in the Stack what he'd found. Two nights later, Nyla's people sealed the deep-forge access and ☽'Ilun stopped being a complication."
    
    "Who did he tell?"
    
    "Ask the someone."
    
    ---
    
    Xueshei's Compass sat in the Qingqe'frostlings' war-hall, wrapped in frost-cloth that did nothing to suppress its hum. When Yunjoushong unwrapped it, the needle locked toward the Nightshelf with enough force to drag the silver-ice frame three finger-widths across the stone. She followed its heading on a chart; it pointed toward the Midnight Claws' fire-core caches exactly as Nyla's confession described. Every route, every vault, every diversion point—the compass confirmed Nyla's account as if the Shadow-Broker had drawn the map herself.
    
    Too precisely.
    
    Yunjoushong laid out the logic for Amaang that evening, in the council chamber, under the aurora's shifting judgments. "Nyla killed ☽'Ilun because the medallion mapped her smuggling routes. She confessed to get ahead of the murder—take the punishment for the smaller crimes before the larger one surfaces. Standard Exchange calculation."
    
    Amaang listened with the stillness of carved ice. "That's consistent. The compass confirms the routes. The courier testimony supports the timeline. You've verified her claims."
    
    "I've verified what she wanted verified."
    
    A silence. In the gallery above, mystics moved with their faint internal glow, the wrong colors, the aurora-grammar that turned every sentence into a celestial equation.
    
    "The approach vectors," Yunjoushong said. "Obscurum~ described routes the medallion showed—approach paths through the eastern ridge. Not smuggling routes. Attack routes."
    
    Amaang's posture didn't change. That was the tell. A penguin hearing about the Shattered-Spire Assaultabout the night seven pods unraveled the Stack's crystal walls—should flinch. Everyone flinched. Even Kael-the-Rememberer, who set out two cups of fire-tea every dawn and drank alone.
    
    "The eastern ridge was breached by Slide Prism," Amaang said. "Orca shamanics. The approach vectors wouldn't matter if—"
    
    "If the defenses were intact. But the medallion showed where they weren't."
    
    Another silence. The aurora shifted from violet to that sickly white-blue.
    
    "That's speculation from a criminal source," Amaang said, and the word *criminal* did the work of a locked door.
    
    Yunjoushong accepted the dismissal. She left the chamber, walked past the Lens-Cathedral, past the enshrined medallion. She didn't stop.
    
    She came back at third-watch, when the aurora had dimmed to its faintest cycle and the Cathedral galleries were empty.
    
    ---
    
    The medallion fit in her closed flipper, heavy enough that she felt it in her shoulder. She carried it down, away from the aurora-light, into the lowest gallery where graffiti scarred the ice supports and the protest-sigils from the Accord years still hadn't been scrubbed. She held it in total darkness.
    
    It glowed.
    
    Not the aurora's colors. Something older, burned into the glass by whatever ice the Dawn Terrace remembered. The pathways shifted, interlocked, separated—and there were the smuggling routes, yes, the ones Nyla had confessed to with such precision, the ones the compass confirmed. Surface map. Obvious map. The map you'd show an auditor who asked the right questions.
    
    She tilted it. Turned it the way ☽'Ilun must have turned it, forcing the glass to yield its underwork. And there—in a light that wasn't light, more like the memory of fire against ice—the second map opened.
    
    Approach vectors through the eastern ridge. Seven paths converging on the Stack's crystalline spires from angles that no orca pod could have found without guidance. Entry points that corresponded not to defensive gaps but to *dismantled* defenses—sections of the crystal barricade that had been weakened from the inside, tool-marks deliberate and careful, made by flippers that knew exactly which lenses to crack to turn the Stack's barriers inside-out before the shamans arrived to finish the work.
    
    And one path that began not in the sea but inside the Stack itself. Inside the council tier. A route marked in the glass with the particular precision of someone who walked it regularly—from the council chambers to the eastern ridge maintenance corridor that had been, the records noted, Amaang Skael'Compass's assigned patrol during the season the Shattered-Spire Assault struck.
    
    Clear skies for the confession; dark skies for the crime.
    
    Nyla had confessed to everything the aurora could judge—smuggling, murder, thirty winters of shadow-brokering—and the confession was real, was verified, was *complete* in exactly the way a sealed crate is complete when you don't check the false bottom. She confessed under clear skies because clear skies were the rule, and the rule meant the medallion sat inert in its shrine, showing nothing, while the council nodded and recorded and the one penguin who needed protecting stood in the council tier accepting the confession with appropriate gravity.
    
    Yunjoushong held the medallion in the dark and felt its weight—the weight of a sealed crate, the weight of what ☽'Ilsancuia Depth-walker had spent a decade trying to heal and ☽'Ilun the Refracted had died for reading aloud. The paths in the glass pulsed once, faintly, as if acknowledging a witness.
    
    She thought of Amaang's posture when the Shattered-Spire Assault was mentioned. The stillness. The geometric control of a penguin who had practiced not flinching.
    
    She thought of Nyla in the Lens-Cathedral, ash-gray wrappings soaking up aurora-light, confessing with the precision of someone who had decided exactly which truths would save her and which truth would save the penguin who had opened the gates.
    
    Somewhere above, the aurora judged all actions. The Exchange counted the cost. And in the lowest gallery of the Stack, the medallion showed its paths to the only penguin watching, and Yunjoushong understood that bringing this to the council meant bringing it to Amaang, and bringing it to Amaang meant the medallion would find its way back to Stillness-Bound and no one would carry it into the dark again.
    
    She wrapped it in her auditor's sash. She took the courier route toward Midway Shelf, where the seventeen skeletons stood as reminder of what happened to those who broke the Accord between civilizations.
    
    She was not breaking an accord. She was carrying evidence past the one authority that would bury it, toward any authority that wouldn't.
    
    The medallion glowed against her chest. Something patient watched from the edge of the dark—not predator, not penguin, something older—and for the first time in her career as an auditor, Yunjoushong did not stop to count what it cost.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771585048875_0", anchor: "The murder of her predecessor—throat opened to match a claw-mark, body fed to a vent", text: "The administrative ice is unambiguous: Nyla's predecessor was Aurora-born, not Nightshelf. The official histories were amended — quietly, competently, in the way that tells you someone with archival access did the amending. An Aurora penguin running the Nightshelf's shadow economy. The implications for the Flipper Accord's power balance are significant enough that I understand why the record was changed. I do not forgive it. The chronicler either didn't know or chose the amended version. Either way, the claw-mark detail is doing work: it makes the killing look like internal Nightshelf business. It wasn't.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771585048875_1", anchor: "where the seventeen skeletons stood in their frost-locked poses", text: "Seventeen again. The couriers walk past these bones every trip and no one has independently verified the count. I would go myself, but my travel funding was among the first casualties of 'forward-facing initiatives.'", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771585048875_2", anchor: "She held it in total darkness", text: "Here is the mechanism the chronicler presents without naming. The medallion's deeper maps — the attack routes, the dismantled defenses — surface only when aurora-light is absent. This replicates exactly what happens when the Long Dark thins the veil between the living world and ice-memory: readings intensify, deeper impressions emerge, the ice yields what it ordinarily suppresses. Nyla chose clear skies for her confession because clear skies kept the medallion blind. The aurora was not judging her actions. It was suppressing the evidence of someone else's.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771585048875_3", anchor: "ice-reading techniques the Stack doesn't teach anymore", text: "Not 'lost.' Suppressed. The Clever Ice Age produced methods that worked beautifully and revealed things institutions preferred stayed frozen. Given what the medallion yielded under those techniques — defense maps, insider routes, the geometric proof of treason — the decision to stop teaching them looks less like safety and more like policy.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771585048875_4", anchor: "the night seven pods unraveled the Stack's crystal walls", text: "The Shattered-Spire Assault belongs to the Orca Incursion — a full era after the Clever Ice Age's peak. The investigation, Kael-the-Rememberer's grief ritual, the assault as solved history rather than approaching threat — all of it places the chronicle's present after the boundary. The Clever Ice Age's energy was industrial: guilds breaching sealed chambers, workshops destabilizing mines. That world is absent. The chronicler wanted a war-crimes noir and set it where the war-crime happened, not where the era's calendar says it should.", type: :temporal, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771585048875_5", anchor: "Amaang Skael'Compass's assigned patrol during the season the Shattered-Spire Assault struck", text: "One penguin's work, the chronicle implies. The eastern ridge maintenance corridor's ice-memory — degraded, but readable if you are patient and I am patient — records tool-marks from multiple flippers over an extended period. Several 'heroes' credited with single-handedly defending colonies during the Incursion were actually covers for coordinated Nightshelf intelligence operations never officially acknowledged. Amaang is one thread. The tapestry is larger and more carefully woven than this chronicle suspects.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771585048875_6", anchor: "Something patient watched from the edge of the dark—not predator, not penguin, something older", text: "Not the Long Dark. Not even close. And yet — presences at perception's edge, patient attention, the unmistakable sense of something pre-penguin. The deep archive holds impressions from beings that predate our civilization entirely; I have seen them and I do not pretend to understand them. Proximity to saturated artifacts in low light can replicate the veil-thinning conditions of the Long Dark locally. The watchers do not need four months of darkness. They need you to be in the dark, holding something that remembers them. Yunjoushong was both.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
